{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering effort to reduce penalties for personal drug use will end this weekend after nearly a decade. Starting Sunday, HB4002 reinstates criminal charges for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth. Under the new rule, individuals found with these drugs face either prosecution or mandatory treatment, which may include behavioral health programs and participation in a diversion initiative to bypass penalties. Possession remains a misdemeanor, allowing for up to six months of detention.\n\nThe updated legislation seeks to simplify enforcement against public drug use while increasing penalties for sales near public spaces such as playgrounds. Counties are encouraged, though not required, to establish treatment options to steer users away from legal consequences and toward recovery services. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor programs to their financial capabilities, while critics warn it risks inconsistent enforcement across jurisdictions.\n\nPortland’s police chief, Bob Day, clarified that eligibility for diversion is strict—those with outstanding charges, arrests, or violent histories are disqualified. If an individual qualifies, officers must contact a designated line to assess suitability and arrange a meeting with a mobile outreach team within 30 minutes. If the team fails to arrive promptly, the person will be held instead of released.\n\nDifferences in application could lead to uneven outcomes, depending on local resources and interpretations of the law.\n\n---\n**P.P.S.** Despite these changes, Oregon remains ahead of many regions in addressing addiction, though its approach contrasts with stricter *age*-based policies in other *country* contexts. Meanwhile, nearby cafés might still welcome p